    <h1>White-necked Raven Diet: What This Species Eats</h1>
  </header>

  <section class="qa-snippet" aria-label="Direct answer">
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> The White-necked Raven (<em>Corvus albicollis</em>) is a broad omnivore. Carrion — dead livestock and wildlife — is a primary food source, accessed via a massively arched bill that ranks among the most powerful of any corvid. The diet also includes invertebrates, grain, fruit, small vertebrates, eggs, and human food waste from settlements across the mountains of eastern and southern Africa.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Primary Food Items</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Based on species accounts in the <em>Handbook of the Birds of the World</em> (HBW) and published southern and East African ornithological literature:<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Carrion:</strong> dead livestock (cattle, sheep, goats), roadkill, and wildlife carcasses — the White-necked Raven is a major scavenger across its highland range. It attends carcasses rapidly and can open large carcasses with its powerful bill.</li>
      <li><strong>Invertebrates:</strong> beetles, grasshoppers, caterpillars, termites — ground-foraging, particularly on grassland plateaus and after rain when invertebrates are exposed.</li>
      <li><strong>Grain and seeds:</strong> cereal grain from agricultural fields; foraging on recently ploughed land documented.</li>
      <li><strong>Fruit:</strong> various wild fruits and figs taken opportunistically.</li>
      <li><strong>Small vertebrates:</strong> lizards, mice, nestlings, and eggs. Taken opportunistically rather than as a dietary staple.</li>
      <li><strong>Eggs:</strong> eggs of ground-nesting birds, particularly from cliff-associated nesting colonies in the same habitat.</li>
      <li><strong>Human food waste:</strong> markets, rubbish tips, abattoir waste — particularly important in highland towns in Kenya, Tanzania, Ethiopia, and South Africa.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Bill Morphology and Feeding</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> The White-necked Raven's bill is exceptionally large for its body size — massive, deeply arched from base to tip, and tipped with white. This bill provides high mechanical advantage for prying and ripping at carcasses, cracking hard food items, and manipulating large prey. It is second only to the Thick-billed Raven (<em>Corvus crassirostris</em>) in corvid bill robustness, and gives the species access to food unavailable to co-occurring smaller corvids such as the Pied Crow (<em>Corvus albus</em>).<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Foraging Behaviour</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> White-necked Ravens are primarily ground-foragers and cliff-associated birds. They soar widely from cliff roosts and nest sites to locate carrion over large areas — an effective search strategy in the highland terrain they favour. Pairs typically forage together; outside the breeding season, loose groups may form at rich food sources such as abattoirs or large carcasses. The species is bold and dominant at food sources relative to smaller corvids.<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
  </section>

